VT_TALK("sql/sql-transactions", {
 "acid": "Moving money from Asha to Ravi takes two updates, and if the power fails in between, money vanishes. A transaction groups statements so they all happen or none do. You write BEGIN, the statements, and COMMIT to make everything permanent at once, or ROLLBACK to throw it all away. Databases promise four things, called ACID. Atomicity means all or nothing. Consistency means every rule still holds afterwards. Isolation means others never see your half done work. Durability means a committed change survives a crash. Without BEGIN, each statement is its own small transaction. Think of a shop till where the sale only counts once the receipt prints.",
 "failure": "When a statement inside a transaction fails, the program decides what happens next. Usually it rolls everything back, so a transfer that cannot finish leaves both accounts as they were. In the animation, pick the failing transfer and watch Ravi receive money, Asha's check fail, and the rollback put everything back. A savepoint is a named marker inside a transaction. ROLLBACK TO that name undoes only what came after it, and the earlier work stays. Most database libraries wrap begin, commit and rollback in one block, so you rarely forget. Think of the undo button in a drawing app that goes back to a checkpoint.",
 "isolation": "Many people use a database at the same moment, and full isolation is expensive, so the SQL standard offers four isolation levels. Each prevents more problems. A dirty read sees someone's change before it commits. A non repeatable read gets a different value the second time. A phantom is a new row that appears when you repeat a search. A lost update happens when two people read the same balance and both write, so one change is overwritten. PostgreSQL and SQL Server default to read committed, MySQL to repeatable read, and SQLite allows one writer at a time. Letting the database do the arithmetic, as in balance equals balance plus one hundred, avoids lost updates. Think of two cashiers updating the same paper ledger.",
 "locks": "Engines keep their promises with two main tools. Locks make other writers wait until a transaction commits. MVCC, multi version concurrency control, keeps old versions of rows, so readers see a consistent snapshot while a writer works, and readers never block writers. SQLite takes a simpler path, with one writer for the whole database at a time. A deadlock happens when two transactions each hold a lock the other needs, and the engine breaks it by cancelling one, which the app should retry. Locking rows in the same order prevents most deadlocks. A write ahead log records changes first, so a crash can be repaired. Think of two people at a narrow door, each waiting for the other to go first.",
 "levels": "Here is the ladder. For a beginner, a transaction is all or nothing. At college level, know ACID, the isolation levels and their problems, savepoints, and how to avoid lost updates. At masters level, serializable means the result matches some one at a time order, two phase locking guarantees it, and snapshot isolation can still allow write skew. At research level, transactions span many machines with two phase commit and consensus, and Google Spanner even uses synchronized clocks. Think of a relay race where the baton is never in two hands at once."
});
